      {
        "id": "s3_epidermis",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Epidermis",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Layer 1",
        "lines": [
          "Epidermis: the outermost protective layer,",
          "single and one cell thick, with thin walls.",
          "The anther splits open at maturity along the",
          "stomium, a strip of specialised epidermal",
          "cells, releasing the pollen."
        ],
        "why": "The epidermis is the boundary layer, and its one special feature is the stomium — the pre-marked line of cells along which the pollen sac finally opens.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing that the epidermis is many-layered — it is a single layer, one cell thick.",
          "Calling the stomium a separate wall layer. It is a region of the epidermis."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Thin skin with a tear-line: the stomium is the perforation along which the anther opens.",
        "margin_note": "Protective, one cell thick, and the stomium — three facts, one mark."
      },
      {
        "id": "s4_endothecium",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Endothecium",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Layer 2",
        "lines": [
          "Endothecium: the hypodermal layer just below",
          "the epidermis.",
          "Its cells expand radially and develop fibrous",
          "thickenings.",
          "At maturity these cells lose water and",
          "contract — this helps the pollen sac dehisce."
        ],
        "why": "The endothecium is the opening mechanism: the fibrous thickenings make the drying cells contract unevenly, and that pull tears the anther open at the stomium.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Giving the fibrous thickenings to the epidermis — they belong to the endothecium.",
          "Stating the thickenings without their job: losing water + contracting = dehiscence."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Endothecium = the spring: fibre bands + drying = the contraction that flips the anther open.",
        "margin_note": "Hypodermal, fibrous thickenings, water loss, dehiscence — chain the four."
      },
      {
        "id": "s5_middle_layers",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Middle layers",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Layer 3",
        "lines": [
          "Middle layers: one to three thin walled",
          "layers lying between the endothecium and the",
          "tapetum.",
          "They are short lived and degenerate as the",
          "anther matures."
        ],
        "why": "The middle layers are defined by position and by being temporary — thin, one to three of them, gone by maturity; a short step because the layer itself is slight.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing a fixed count. The book says 1-3 layers — give the range.",
          "Making them thick walled. Thin walls are their identifying feature."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "The middle child of the wall: 1-3 thin layers, squeezed between two famous ones, gone early.",
        "margin_note": "Position + count + thin walled — one compact sentence carries the mark."
      },
      {
        "id": "s6_tapetum",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Tapetum",
        "marks": 2,
        "mark_note": "Layer 4",
        "lines": [
          "Tapetum: the innermost wall layer, one cell",
          "thick, and nutritive in function.",
          "Its cells possess dense cytoplasm and are",
          "binucleate.",
          "It nourishes the developing sporogenous",
          "tissue (the microspore mother cells)."
        ],
        "why": "The tapetum earns double weight because it does the biology: pressed against the sporogenous tissue, its dense-cytoplasm, two-nucleus cells feed the developing microspores.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Placing the tapetum outside the middle layers. It is the INNERMOST layer.",
          "Writing uninucleate cells. Binucleate (often multinucleate) cells are its signature.",
          "Calling it protective. Its function is nutrition."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Tapetum = the kitchen layer: innermost, dense cytoplasm, two nuclei, feeds the mother cells.",
        "margin_note": "Position, one-cell thickness, dense cytoplasm, binucleate, nutrition — the full 2-mark list."
      },
